# Site parameters for the Kerberized MCP bridge. PLACEHOLDERS ONLY.
#
# This file is committed. It must never contain a real hostname, realm, email
# address or any other site value. Copy it, fill in the copy, and keep the copy
# out of the repository:
#
#     install -d -m 0750 -o root -g root /etc/mcp-server
#     install -m 0640 -o root -g root server/install/site.env.example /etc/mcp-server/site.env
#     $EDITOR /etc/mcp-server/site.env          # on the MCP host
#
# ORDERING, and it matters on a fresh host: these commands run BEFORE
# run.sh, and the mcp group does not exist yet. run.sh
# step 2 is what creates it. An earlier version of this header said
# `-g mcp` here, so the very first documented command on a brand new host
# failed with "invalid group". Create the directory root:root; the installer
# re-owns it to root:$SVCGROUP in step 5, once the account exists.
#
# The 0750 mode on /etc/mcp-server is the keytab contract, not a suggestion:
# that directory also holds krb5.keytab, which is 0640 root:<SVCGROUP> in
# direct mode so the service account can read it. Creating the directory 0755
# by hand first would leave it world-traversable, and `install -d` does NOT
# change the mode of a directory that already exists, so the installer would
# not repair it. Creating it 0750 root:root is safe in the other direction:
# too tight is repaired by the installer, too loose is not.
#
# That path is gitignored, as is any file literally named site.env.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: before it, each script carried its own flag set and the
# operator hand-edited the same handful of values in seven separate files. That
# hand-editing is the manual remediation the installers are meant to remove.
# One source of truth means one place to get it wrong, and one place to check.
#
# HOW SCRIPTS CONSUME IT: every script resolves values in the order
#     command-line flag  >  sourced site.env  >  hard error
# There is deliberately no fallback to the placeholder values below. A script
# that finds a required variable empty, or still set to example.internal or
# EXAMPLE.INTERNAL, must exit non-zero and name the variable. Building a host
# against example.internal is a worse outcome than refusing to run, because it
# fails later, on someone else's machine, with a DNS error that points nowhere
# near the cause.
#
# This is sourced by POSIX sh. Keep it to plain KEY=value assignments. Quote
# any value containing a space. Do not add logic, command substitution or
# anything with a side effect: several scripts source it while running as root.
#
# THE INVARIANT, and it is the whole point of the file: THIS FILE IS THE
# COMPLETE CONFIG SURFACE. Every variable any script in this repository reads
# from the environment or from site.env appears below, with its default, even
# when that default is right for every site. Nothing is discoverable only by
# reading the scripts.
#
# It was not always true, and the failure it produced is the reason the rule is
# written down. An installer died with "Set CA_CRT in <site.env>", naming a
# variable this file had never mentioned, so the operator was told to edit a key
# that did not exist in the documented config. Others were readable by the
# scripts and absent here for the same reason: they had working defaults, so
# nothing broke on a default install and nobody noticed until somebody needed to
# change one. The rule cuts both ways: when a feature is removed, its keys leave
# this file together with the code that read them.
#
# WHEN YOU ADD A `${SOMETHING:-default}` READ TO ANY SCRIPT, ADD IT HERE.
# The reverse check matters too: a variable defined here that no script reads is
# a lie about what is configurable, so delete it or mark it explicitly unused.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- identity --

# DNS domain of the realm, lowercase. NOT READ BY ANY SCRIPT IN THIS REPOSITORY
# any more; it is kept because it is the value every other identity value below
# has to agree with, and an operator filling this file in needs it written down.
# Marked explicitly rather than deleted, per the invariant above.
DOMAIN=example.internal

# Kerberos realm, uppercase. Never inferred from DOMAIN by any script: sites do
# exist where the realm is not the uppercased domain, and guessing produces a
# keytab that looks correct and authenticates nothing.
REALM=EXAMPLE.INTERNAL

# Fully qualified name of the host running the MCP server. This is the name in
# the HTTP/ service principal, the nginx server_name and the certificate, so it
# must match forward and reverse DNS exactly.
MCP_HOST=mcp.example.internal

# FreeIPA server used for enrolment, keytab retrieval and ACME issuance.
IPA_SERVER=ipa.example.internal

# -------------------------------------------------------------------- TLS --

# ACME directory endpoint. This is the realm's OWN FreeIPA/Dogtag CA, not a
# public CA. A .internal name has no public suffix, so no public CA can ever
# issue for it. Enable the responder once per realm with:
#     ipa-acme-manage enable
ACME_DIRECTORY=https://ipa.example.internal/acme/directory

# Contact address registered with the ACME account. Renewal failure notices go
# here. Leave empty only if the site accepts an unregistered account.
ACME_EMAIL=it@example.com

# RSA key size for issued certificates. RSA is MANDATORY here: certbot 2.x
# defaults to ECDSA and the Dogtag ACME profile rejects it at finalize, with an
# error that does not name the key type. Installers pass --key-type rsa and
# this size explicitly.
ACME_RSA_KEY_SIZE=4096

# certbot lineage name, which fixes the /etc/letsencrypt/live/<name>/ path that
# the nginx config references. Keep it equal to the host name unless the site
# has a reason otherwise: a mismatch means certbot renews into a directory the
# vhost does not read, and nginx keeps serving the old certificate until it
# expires.
MCP_CERT_NAME=mcp.example.internal

# Docroot used for the http-01 challenge. Must be the same path the port 80
# nginx block exposes at /.well-known/acme-challenge/, and that location must
# not be redirected to HTTPS: behind the Kerberos gate the redirect target
# answers 401 and renewal fails silently.
WEBROOT=/var/www/html

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- MCP host --

# Python virtualenv for the server. It MUST be created with
# --system-site-packages: python3-gssapi is an OS package shipped with the IPA
# client and is not reliably pip-installable here, while mcp and uvicorn are
# pip-only. An isolated venv yields a server that starts and then fails every
# SPNEGO handshake with an import error buried in the journal.
MCP_VENV=/opt/mcp-venv

# Unprivileged service account the unit runs as, created by run.sh.
# The keytab is readable by this group, which is why the group matters.
SVCUSER=mcp
SVCGROUP=mcp

# systemd unit name for the MCP server. Read by server/install/verify.sh, which
# discovers the SPN, the socket path and the service account FROM the unit, so
# a renamed unit makes the verifier check a service that does not exist and
# report a wall of failures about the wrong machine. Change this only if you
# also renamed server/install/mcp-server.service.
MCP_UNIT=mcp-server

# ------------------------------------------------- compatibility aliases --
#
# Two naming conventions for the same two values are in use across the scripts:
# MCP_HOST/IPA_SERVER above, and MCP_FQDN/IPA_FQDN in verify.sh and in
# the client scripts. Both are defined here from the single set of values above
# so that whichever name a script reads, it gets the same answer.
#
# This matters because the scripts hard-error on an empty required variable.
# Without these lines a correctly filled-in site.env would abort with
# "MCP_FQDN is empty" while MCP_HOST sits right there, set correctly, which is a
# confusing failure to debug at 2am. Plain expansions only, no command
# substitution, so this stays safe to source as root.
#
# MCP_HOST and IPA_SERVER are CANONICAL; MCP_FQDN and IPA_FQDN are accepted
# aliases. Prefer setting the canonical names here.
#
# The scripts no longer depend on these two lines being present or on their
# direction. run.sh resolves the pairs BIDIRECTIONALLY immediately
# after sourcing this file, so setting either name of a pair populates the
# other. The lines below only mirror that for a site.env that is read by
# something else. They were one-directional once, and a site.env that set only
# MCP_FQDN aborted run.sh with "no FQDN" while the value sat right
# there in the file.
MCP_FQDN="${MCP_FQDN:-$MCP_HOST}"
IPA_FQDN="${IPA_FQDN:-$IPA_SERVER}"

# --- on-behalf-of forwarding (SECURITY.md [D1]) ------------------------------
# OFF unless set. When empty the acceptor stays receive-only, no evidence
# credential is ever requested, and the server behaves exactly as it did before
# this feature existed.
#
# Setting it to 1 is a SECURITY DECISION, not a tuning knob. It narrows [C2] (the
# keytab becomes usable for outbound authentication) and it raises what a stolen
# keytab is worth, which is the risk [K1] already accepts against a smaller
# prize. Read both before enabling.
#
# It also needs, all outside this file:
#   - a FreeIPA servicedelegationrule binding HTTP/$MCP_HOST to a target list
#   - callers holding FORWARDABLE tickets (workstation krb5.conf forwardable=true)
#   - a target named below for every tool that forwards
MCP_DELEGATION=

# Where each forwarding tool is allowed to reach, 'tool=svc@fqdn' entries
# separated by commas. Empty means no tool may forward anywhere, which is the
# shipped state and is why enabling MCP_DELEGATION alone changes nothing
# observable: every forward still fails closed with no-target-policy.
#
#   MCP_DELEGATION_TARGETS=trigger_build=HTTP@ci.example.internal
#
# TREAT THIS LINE AS THE SECURITY BOUNDARY IT IS. It is the control a reviewer
# can actually read, and the one that says where a tool may reach.
#
# The realm's own servicedelegationtarget allowlist backs it up, and that backing
# is real: a caller who sets GSS_C_DELEG_FLAG would hand this server a full
# forwarded TGT rather than a narrow evidence credential, which the KDC does NOT
# constrain, and the server refuses exactly that credential
# (delegation.is_narrow_evidence). So the KDC's list applies to everything that
# is actually used. Both were demonstrated on a live KDC; see SECURITY.md [D1].
# Two controls, not one, but review this line as though it were the only one.
#
# FQDNs only, never short names, so a resolver cannot widen the target. One
# target per tool: several would mean something has to choose at runtime, and
# the only inputs available then come from the caller. A tool named here that
# does not call forward_header is a live grant sitting unused; a test fails the
# build on that, and run.sh refuses to install it.
MCP_DELEGATION_TARGETS=

# --- your own tools ----------------------------------------------------------
# Absolute path to a Python file holding this deployment's tools. Empty means
# the server serves only the four stubs this repository ships.
#
# The file defines one function and nothing else is required of it:
#
#     from mcp.server.fastmcp import Context
#
#     def register(mcp, require, forward_header, register_tool_policy):
#         @mcp.tool()
#         def list_tickets(ctx: Context) -> str:
#             """List the caller's tickets."""
#             p = require(ctx, 'list_tickets')          # authorize first, always
#             h = forward_header(ctx, 'list_tickets')   # optional: act as the caller
#             ...
#         register_tool_policy('list_tickets', {'support-staff'})
#
# Three rules run.sh enforces before it will install, each for a reason:
#
#   Not inside the code directory. run.sh converges that directory on this
#   repository's file set, so a file left there is deleted on the next install
#   and the tools vanish. /etc/mcp-server/ is the natural home.
#
#   Owned by root, not group- or world-writable. It is imported by the service,
#   so whoever can write it can execute code as the service, and that process
#   holds the keytab. Edit it somewhere else and install it root-owned.
#
#   Valid Python. A syntax error here stops the unit at startup.
#
# A forwarding tool defined here still needs its row in MCP_DELEGATION_TARGETS
# above; run.sh looks in this file as well as mcp_server.py when checking that
# every target names a tool that actually forwards.
MCP_SITE_TOOLS=

# --- client bundle -----------------------------------------------------------
# This host serves the client bundle (the five files setup.sh, setup.ps1,
# install-bridge.sh, JsoncEdit.ps1, mcp-krb-bridge.py, plus the provisioning page) at
# CLIENT_PATH BY DEFAULT. A fresh install needs nothing here.
#
# The bundle is served WITHOUT authentication, necessarily: a machine being
# provisioned has no Kerberos ticket yet, so anything it must fetch in order to
# get one cannot demand one. These are provisioning scripts, not secrets, and
# nothing is signed; see SECURITY.md [SC1].
#
#   SERVE_CLIENT=no   do not serve it here. Use this if you distribute the bundle
#                     another way (an internal repo, MDM, a base image). Export a
#                     copy with CLIENT_EXPORT below and serve it wherever you like;
#                     the page infers its own download URL, so no per-host config.
SERVE_CLIENT=yes

# Also assemble the bundle into a local folder, to copy off-band to another host.
# Independent of SERVE_CLIENT. Absolute path, empty means "do not export".
CLIENT_EXPORT=

# Where the bundle is served. The path element and the last element of the
# docroot must be the SAME WORD, because nginx appends the URI to root. The
# installer refuses a mismatch rather than serving a 404 from a vhost that looks
# correct.
CLIENT_PATH=/client/
CLIENT_ROOT=/var/www/client

# Cosmetic page values. All optional and derived from the install when unset:
# the org name defaults to the domain, the macOS resolver IP to this host's own.
CLIENT_ORG_NAME=
CLIENT_SUPPORT_EMAIL=
CLIENT_DNS_IP=
